module

public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IntermediateField.Adjoin.Defs
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Defs
public import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Finsupp.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.CharP.Defs

/-!
# Extension of p-independent families to p-bases

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Definition 15.47.1 (Tag 07P1) and
Lemma 15.47.2 (Tag 07P2), the p-basis extension assertion
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-p-independent-extends-to-p-basis).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace FieldTheory

/-- A p-independent family extends to a p-basis, preserving its original indexing.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tags 07P1 and 07P2
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-p-independent-extends-to-p-basis).

The coefficient field is the intermediate field generated by k and the p-th powers in K.
Bounded natural-valued finitely supported functions index the monomials, including 1.

Proof:
1. Independence implies the given family is injective: two equal entries give a relation
   between distinct single-variable monomials, also when p = 2. Identify it with its image S₀.
2. Order the p-independent subsets containing S₀ by inclusion. A chain union is independent,
   since each finite relation uses finitely many generators, all in one chain member.
   The empty chain has upper bound S₀. Apply lem-weil-pbc-zorn-lemma to obtain a maximal T.
3. The monomial span L is a subfield. Reduce exponents modulo p in products, absorbing p-th
   powers into coefficients; for nonzero a, its inverse is a^(p-1) / a^p. Thus L is also the
   field generated by the coefficient field and T.
4. If x is outside L, x^p belongs to L, so x is algebraic
   (def-weil-algebraic-element). By lem-weil-pbc-simple-algebraic-extension-power-basis,
   the minimal polynomial has degree d = [L(x):L]. It divides X^p - x^p = (X-x)^p,
   hence is (X-x)^d. If 1 ≤ d < p, its next coefficient -d*x puts x in L, a contradiction.
   Thus d = p, and the first p powers of x are independent over L.
   This uses X^p - x^p, correcting the source proof's misprint X^p - x.
5. Group any relation for T ∪ {x} by the exponent of x. Independence of its powers over L,
   followed by independence of T's monomials, makes all coefficients zero, contradicting
   maximality. Therefore L = K.
6. Take J = T ∖ S₀ with its inclusion y into K. The original family and y give a bijection
   I ⊕ J → T. Reindex the monomials: they remain independent and span K, hence form a basis.
-/
theorem exists_pBasis_extension
    {k : Type u} {K : Type v} [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] [CharP k p] [CharP K p]
    {I : Type w} (x : I → K)
    (hx : LinearIndependent (IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range (fun a : K ↦ a ^ p)))
      (fun E : {E : I →₀ ℕ // ∀ i : I, E i < p} ↦ E.val.prod (fun i e ↦ x i ^ e))) :
    ∃ (J : Type v) (y : J → K)
      (b : Module.Basis {E : (I ⊕ J) →₀ ℕ // ∀ l : I ⊕ J, E l < p}
        (IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range (fun a : K ↦ a ^ p))) K),
      ∀ E, b E = E.val.prod (fun l e ↦ Sum.elim x y l ^ e) := by
  sorry

end FieldTheory
